{"schema_version":"1.9.2","id":"SUSE-SU-2026:5064-1","published":"2026-11-03T07:32:38Z","modified":"2026-11-03T22:45:04.564059533Z","related":["CVE-2026-101917","CVE-2026-101918","CVE-2026-102267","CVE-2026-102268","CVE-2026-102269","CVE-2026-102270","CVE-2026-102271","CVE-2026-102275"],"upstream":["CVE-2026-101917","CVE-2026-101918","CVE-2026-102267","CVE-2026-102268","CVE-2026-102269","CVE-2026-102270","CVE-2026-102271","CVE-2026-102275"],"summary":"Security update for python-PyJWT","details":"This update for python-PyJWT fixes the following issues:\n\n- CVE-2026-101917: attacker traffic can amplify outbound requests to the configured JWKS endpoint (bsc#1283060).\n- CVE-2026-101918: unauthenticated request can raise an exception that may produce an HTTP 500 response (bsc#1283061).\n- CVE-2026-102267: forwarded credentials may be disclosed or verification keys may be substituted (bsc#1283064).\n- CVE-2026-102268: an attacker who knows the public key can forge authenticated HMAC tokens (bsc#1283065).\n- CVE-2026-102269: raw-token revocation checks can fail to recognize an equivalent modified token (bsc#1283066).\n- CVE-2026-102270: an attacker can cause intensive CPU consumption (bsc#1283067).\n- CVE-2026-102271: an attacker who knows the public key can forge authenticated HMAC tokens (bsc#1283068).\n- CVE-2026-102275: an attacker may use a stolen sender-constrained token without the legitimate private key\n  (bsc#1283072).\n","affected":[{"package":{"name":"python-PyJWT","ecosystem":"SUSE:Linux Enterprise Module for Public Cloud 15 SP4","purl":"pkg:rpm/suse/python-PyJWT&distro=SUSE%20Linux%20Enterprise%20Module%20for%20Public%20Cloud%2015%20SP4"},"ranges":[{"type":"ECOSYSTEM","events":[{"introduced":"0"},{"fixed":"2.8.0-150400.8.20.1"}]}],"ecosystem_specific":{"binaries":[{"python311-PyJWT":"2.8.0-150400.8.20.1"}]},"database_specific":{"source":"https://ftp.suse.com/pub/projects/security/osv/SUSE-SU-2026:5064-1.json"}},{"package":{"name":"python-PyJWT","ecosystem":